TASKS = {
"regex": '''Write `regex_match(pattern: str, s: str) -> bool`, true when the whole of s matches pattern (a full match), without using the re module. Syntax: alternation "|" (lowest precedence; empty alternatives are allowed, e.g. "a|" matches "" and "a"), concatenation, postfix "*", "+", "?" (which may follow any atom or another quantifier), groups "(...)" (possibly empty), "." matching any one character, character classes "[...]" with ranges like "a-z", negation by a leading "^", "]" taken literally when it is the first character of the class (after any "^"), "-" literal when first or last in the class; a backslash makes the next character literal, inside or outside classes. Every other character matches itself. Raise ValueError on an invalid pattern: unbalanced parentheses, a quantifier with nothing before it (e.g. "*a", "(*)", "a|*"), a trailing backslash, an unclosed class, or a range whose end is before its start. Matching must take time polynomial in the lengths: patterns such as "(a|aa)*b" or "(a*)*b" against thousands of characters must finish in well under a second.''',
"sudoku": '''Write `sudoku(grid: str) -> str | None`: grid is 81 characters, rows from top to bottom, each a digit 1-9 or "." for an empty cell. Return the solved grid as 81 digits, or None if it has no solution (including when the given digits already conflict). Puzzles given will have at most one solution. It must solve very hard minimal puzzles (around 17-26 clues, designed to defeat naive backtracking) in well under a second each.''',
"linsolve": '''Write `linsolve(A: list[list[int]], b: list[int])` solving A x = b exactly over the rationals (use fractions.Fraction). A has m rows and n columns (m, n >= 1). Return ("unique", x) when there is exactly one solution; ("none", None) when there is none; ("infinite", x) when there are infinitely many, where x is the particular solution obtained from the reduced row echelon form with every free variable set to 0 (the pivot columns are the leftmost possible: a column is a pivot column exactly when it is not a linear combination of the columns to its left). x is a list of n Fractions.''',
"utf8": '''Write `utf8_decode(b: bytes) -> str` decoding UTF-8 without using bytes.decode, str(), codecs or any other built-in decoder. Valid sequences decode normally (no overlong forms, no surrogates U+D800-U+DFFF, nothing above U+10FFFF). Invalid input is replaced by U+FFFD using the "maximal subpart" practice of the Unicode Standard (the same as the WHATWG Encoding Standard): each maximal subpart of an ill-formed sequence (the longest prefix of a valid sequence that could still have been completed) becomes one U+FFFD, and a byte that cannot start any such prefix becomes one U+FFFD on its own.''',
"csv": '''Write `parse_csv(text: str) -> list[list[str]]` without the csv module. Records end at "\\n" or "\\r\\n" (a lone "\\r" is ordinary data); fields are separated by ",". A field that begins with a double quote is quoted: it runs to the next double quote not doubled ("" inside it stands for one "), may contain commas, "\\r" and newlines, and must be followed directly by a comma, a record end or the end of the text, otherwise raise ValueError; an unterminated quoted field raises ValueError. In an unquoted field a double quote is an ordinary character. A final record end does not create an extra record; the empty text gives []; a line that is empty gives the record [""].''',
"lattice": '''Write `lattice_paths(h: int, w: int, blocked: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> int`: the number of paths from (0, 0) to (h, w) moving one step at a time either (row+1) or (col+1), never stepping on a blocked point, modulo 1_000_000_007. Points are (row, col). If (0, 0) or (h, w) is blocked the answer is 0. Blocked points outside the rectangle, or repeated, have no effect. h and w are up to 200000 and there are up to 2000 blocked points; it must finish in a few seconds.''',
"maxflow": '''Write `max_flow(n: int, edges: list[tuple[int, int, int]], s: int, t: int) -> int`: the value of a maximum flow from s to t in a directed graph with nodes 0..n-1 and edges (u, v, capacity) with non-negative integer capacities; parallel edges and edges in both directions may appear, and self-loops are allowed (and useless). Raise ValueError if s == t. Graphs have up to 500 nodes and 5000 edges; it must finish in a few seconds.''',
"approx": '''Write `best_approx(x: fractions.Fraction, N: int) -> fractions.Fraction`: the fraction p/q with 1 <= q <= N closest to x; among equally close ones, the one with the smallest denominator, and then the smaller of those. N may be as large as 10**18, so it must be fast.''',
"edit": '''Write `edit_script(a: str, b: str) -> list[tuple]` returning a shortest sequence of operations turning a into b, read left to right through a: ("keep", c) keeps the next character c of a; ("sub", c, d) replaces the next character c of a with d (c != d); ("del", c) deletes the next character c of a; ("ins", d) inserts d. The output must consist of exactly the operations, in order, such that applying them to a yields b, and the number of non-"keep" operations must be the minimum possible (the Levenshtein distance). Strings may be up to 2000 characters long.''',
"easter": '''Write `easter(year: int) -> datetime.date`: the date of Easter Sunday in the Gregorian calendar (Western churches) for any year from 1583 to 9999.''',
"duration": '''Write `add_duration(dt: datetime.datetime, iso: str) -> datetime.datetime` adding an ISO 8601 duration of the form [-]P[nY][nM][nW][nD][T[nH][nM][nS]] where each n is a non-negative integer, the components appear in that order, at least one is present, and "T" appears only if followed by at least one time component. Apply years and months first, as a calendar step: move to the same day in the target month, clamped to that month's last day (2024-01-31 + P1M = 2024-02-29). Then add weeks, days, hours, minutes and seconds as exact elapsed time. A leading "-" negates every component, applied in the same order (2024-03-31 - P1M = 2024-02-29). Raise ValueError for anything else, or if the result falls outside the years 1 to 9999.''',
"window": '''Write `min_window(s: str, t: str) -> str`: the shortest substring of s that contains every character of t at least as many times as it occurs in t; among the shortest, the leftmost; "" if there is none or if t is empty. s may be 10**6 characters long, so it must be linear time.''',
}
